title: Distances in the Scene view
intro: Drag how far a light reaches, or how close an enemy notices you, right in the Scene view.
project: TopDown | Scene Distances

create: Light
rename: Lamp
set: Transform.Position = 20, 0, 0
set: Light.Radius = 3
deselect
create: Sprite
rename: Guard
set: Transform.Position = 32, 0, 0
set: Sprite.Tint = #FF004D
add: Chase
add: Shooter
set: Shooter.Target = Player
deselect

chapter: Distance handles | View > Distance Handles, or D
select: Lamp
look: 20, 0, 5
say: A selected light shows how far it reaches as a dashed circle. Until now, the only way to change it was typing in the Inspector.
menu: View > Distance Handles
explain: handle:Light.Radius | Distance handles | With distance handles out, each circle gets a knob, with its name beside it.
distance: Ctrl+Light.Radius = 5
expectvalue: Lamp.Light.Radius = 5
explain: field:Light.Radius | Radius | Drag the knob and the radius follows. Holding control snaps it to half units.

chapter: Ranges | Every distance on the entity
select: Guard
look: 32, 0, 11
say: This guard has two circles: how close the player has to come before it gives chase, and how far it shoots.
explain: handle:Chase.Range | Chase range | Each has its own knob, spaced around the circle so they don't hide each other.
distance: Ctrl+Chase.Range = 4
expectvalue: Guard.Chase.Range = 4
distance: Ctrl+Shooter.Range = 7.5
expectvalue: Guard.Shooter.Range = 7.5
explain: field:Shooter.Range | Range | So a sneaky level is tuned by eye: pull the guard's notice in, push its aim out.
key: Ctrl+Z
expectvalue: Guard.Shooter.Range = 10
say: Each drag is one step to undo. Light radii, sound ranges, a unit's sight and your own scripts' distances all get knobs the same way.
menu: View > Scale Handles
